is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r7
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: FRR OSPF daemon on R7 (the “routing brain” for the R7 region). Mechanism: R7’s OSPF process restarted/reloaded, which briefly tore down the adjacency to its upstream (R6) on link Y. While the adjacency was down, R7’s LSAs (and the summaries it contributes) were withdrawn, so upstream routers (via R3 to R1) lost all the R7-region routes (e.g., 17.x.x.x, 67.0.0.0/8, 70.0.0.0/8), triggering the monitoring red state. Localization: On R7, OSPF neighbor to 16.1.4.1 (R6) on interface eth0 (67.1.1.2) shows Up Time ≈1 minute, indicating a recent flap. R7’s frr.log records “OSPF: Configuration Read in” at 01:02:17 and “Neighbor … Negotiation done” at 01:03:02, confirming an OSPF process restart/reload. Corroboration from R1: routes to 67.0.0.0/8 and 70.0.0.0/8 are again present and were “Last update ~00:01:40 ago,” aligning with the R7 flap; reachability to 67.1.1.2 from R1 now succeeds. Root cause: An unintended FRR/OSPF restart or config reload on R7 caused its OSPF adjacency to reset, temporarily withdrawing the R7-region routes from the network. Corrective fix: No action needed to restore service (it has auto-recovered), but prevent recurrence by investigating why FRR restarted on R7 (automation/cron, manual change, crash), disable unscheduled reloads, and consider adding BFD or OSPF fast timers to reduce convergence impact and tightening change controls/alerts around FRR restarts. === END ===